data.content:
Yes, C# fully supports `stackalloc` with native-sized integers. Both `Span<nint>` and `Span<nuint>` work:

```csharp
Span<nint>  nints  = stackalloc nint[5];
Span<nuint> nuints = stackalloc nuint[3];
```

On this 64-bit system each element is 8 bytes. On a 32-bit runtime they'd be 4 bytes. This has been supported since C# 9 / .NET 5 when `nint`/`nuint` were introduced as first-class types (aliases for `System.IntPtr`/`System.UIntPtr`).
